.TH RENDER 9
.SH NAME
render \- choose how a border reaches the page
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f5render\fP ?\f5direct\fP|\f5scene\fP|\f5count\fP?
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Render
sets which drawing path a flat border takes, and reports which is in
force when asked with no argument.
.TP
\f5direct\fP
Straight to
.IR draw (2),
as this toolkit has always done.
This is the default.
.TP
\f5scene\fP
By building a render-node tree and rendering that.
.TP
\f5count\fP
Answers three numbers: how many trees the repaints since the last
\f5render count\fP rendered, how many widgets went into them, and how
many nodes those trees held.
Counting then starts again.
Batching cannot be seen in the pixels \(em that is the point of it \(em
so this is what a test reads instead.
The node count is what sizes a backend: a submission per tree is worth
making only if a tree carries enough nodes to pay for one.
The pane in
.IR tkofftest (1)
averages a couple of dozen, and prints the figure when it runs rather
than having it repeated here, where it goes stale every time a widget
is converted.
.PP
It exists to be compared against itself.
A node tree is the first piece of a renderer shaped like GTK4's GSK \(em
a retained description of a frame that something other than an immediate
.I draw
can consume \(em and the only thing that makes one worth having before a
GPU backend exists is the ability to check that it draws the same pixels.
The arrangement is the one
.IR draw3d (2)
already uses, where a software provider and a device provider are
compared against each other by
.IR sprite2dtest (1)
rather than each being trusted alone.
.PP
So this is not a setting a program has any reason to change.
It is a whole-process switch, not a property of a toplevel, because a
test wants both and an application wants neither.
.PP
.IR Tkofftest (1)
selects each in turn, reads back the offscreen pane, and requires the two
to agree byte for byte.
.SH SOURCE
.B /libtk/scene.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR style (9),
.IR tkofftest (1),
.IR tkbench (1)
.SH BUGS
Nine things go through it: a flat border, a plain
.IR button (9)
with text, a
.IR radiobutton (9)
with an indicator and text, an
.IR entry (9),
selection and
.B -show
included, a
.IR listbox (9),
which needs no exception at all \(em everything it draws is a filled
rectangle, a run of text or a stroked box \(em a
.IR scale (9)
in either orientation, a
.IR scrollbar (9),
and a
.IR label (9),
which is the widget there are most of and which brings
.IR menubutton (9)
and
.IR choicebutton (9)
with it, since one draw method serves all three, and a
.IR checkbutton (9)
in either state.
Each of those draws its whole interior as nodes \(em fill, glyphs, caret,
selection band, focus ring, border \(em inside one
.BR TkNlayer .
.PP
Everything else \(em images, the classic two-tone bevel, a
.IR text (9),
and the chrome that
.IR scale (9)
and
.IR scrollbar (9)
draw for themselves \(em still draws directly, so a scene is at present a
description of part of a frame rather than of a frame.
.PP
The tree is built and thrown away on every repaint, so none of the
benefit of a RETAINED tree is realised: nothing is diffed, nothing is
reused, and no animation is cheaper than it was.
What exists is the vocabulary and a renderer for it.
.PP
.B TkNborder
is a pure stroke and takes no colour for what shows outside its corners,
because a widget described entirely by nodes never needs one: its fill is
a
.B TkNroundfill
and the corner is simply never painted.
A
.I frame
works that way now.
.PP
No widget needs that any more.
A
.B TkNlayer
carries a corner radius and its composite takes that shape, so a
widget's corners are simply not copied and
.B TkNcorners
is emitted by nothing.
The type and its renderer stay, because a node type's number must not
move once a backend has seen it.
.PP
Both paths end in the same shaped blit
.RI ( tkshapedblit ,
with
.I tkinset
and
.I tkclamprad
shared so that the fill, the border and the blit cannot disagree about
where the curve is).
A widget draws itself square into a scratch and the shape is applied
when the result is copied out, so the corners of the destination are
left as they were \(em which is whatever is behind the widget.
Painting them with the parent's flat background, which is what this did
before, is right only while that is what is there: a bordered
.IR entry (9)
over a red
.IR canvas (9)
item had its corners painted page-grey over the red.
.PP
The border stroke is clamped to that shape too, in both paths.
Unclamped it draws outside the outline it has just cut away, which is
invisible until a widget is narrower than twice its own corner \(em an
8-pixel entry, a thin scrollbar \(em and then two pixels of border stick
out of a row that is otherwise not the widget at all.
.PP
The shape belongs on the composite rather than on the fill, and that
was established by trying the fill first: a rounded
.B TkNroundfill
under a square composite still showed a
.IR listbox (9)'s
selection band in the corner, because the band is drawn after the fill
and runs the full width of the widget.
Twelve pixels.
.PP
It also required the focus ring to be drawn BEFORE the border in
.IR entry (9)
and
.IR scale (9),
which is the order the other widgets already used.
A ring drawn after the border puts its square corner where the border
has just cut the widget away, so a focused entry showed a blue notch
outside its own rounded corner \(em in the ordinary drawing path, not
just in the nodes.
The border is the last word on the shape of a widget.
.PP
A
.B TkNlayer
renders the nodes that follow it into a scratch surface and blits the
result where they would have gone.
Layers do not nest, and that is not a simplification: a frame takes 27
scratch surfaces but
.I tkitmp
hands out one cached scratch per context, reused and blitted out between
widgets, so two live layers would be the same image.
One saved target is therefore sufficient.
.PP
It was proved on a
.IR frame (9),
which does not need one \(em it draws straight into its parent's image.
That was the point: the result had to be identical to rendering the same
nodes in place, so an error in the scratch's extent or the blit's offset
showed as a difference rather than as a widget that looked fine.  Every
widget that genuinely needs a layer draws with something other than
nodes, so none of them could have told us whether it worked.
.PP
A frame does not have one now, and the reason is a measurement.
A layer is a scratch the size of the widget, allocated, filled and
blitted every repaint, and a frame is usually the size of the window:
.IR tkbench (1)
put an empty toplevel \(em two frames \(em at 1.41ms on the node path
against 59us direct, and taking the layer off the frame put it back to
114us.
With that gone, each widget's node path costs what its direct path
costs, to within the noise of the measurement.
The six widgets that genuinely need a layer keep the node exercised.
.PP
The one shape in the flat style that is not a rectangle is a
.IR radiobutton (9)'s
indicator, a ring with a dot in it, and the flat
.IR scale (9)'s
knob, which is two filled circles.
.B TkNellipse
and
.B TkNellipsefill
exist for those.
They use the fields differently from every other node \(em
.B r.min
is the centre and
.B sp
is the two radii \(em because an ellipse is a centre and two radii, and
a bounding rectangle cannot say whether a circle of radius
.I r
is
.I 2r
or
.I 2r+1
across.
They are numbered after the six below rather than beside their
rectangular cousins, because the numbering of a node type must not move
once a backend has seen it.
.PP
The flat
.IR scrollbar (9)'s
arrows are solid triangles, the one shape left in the flat style that is
neither a rectangle nor an ellipse, and
.B TkNpolyfill
exists for them.
Its points are copied into the scene, as a glyph node's string is, and
it always fills by the nonzero winding rule.
.PP
A
.IR checkbutton (9)'s
tick is a stroked B-spline, which is the last thing in the flat style
that no other node could describe, and
.B TkNspline
exists for it.
It owns a copy of its points as
.B TkNpolyfill
does;
.B width
is the thickness and
.B radius
carries the end style, the same one at both ends, which is what every
caller asks for.
.PP
A repaint builds one tree for each RUN of consecutive widgets that can
describe themselves as nodes, not one per widget and not one for the
whole repaint.
.I tkdrawslaves
opens a walk round its descent;
.I tkwalkjoin
hands a widget the walk's tree if one is open for that context and that
window, and
.I tkscenefinish
renders a tree only if it is not the walk's.
Anything that paints into the window itself \(em every widget that asks
.I tkitmp
for the scratch surface, and the canvas, panel, text and menu painters,
which draw straight into it \(em calls
.I tkwalkflush
first, or the tree, rendered later, would land on top of what it drew.
So does the grid clipping in
.IR tkdrawslaves1 ,
whose narrowed clip rectangle is gone by the time the nodes would
otherwise be rendered.
Descents nest \(em a canvas's window item and a text widget's embedded
windows each descend from inside a draw method \(em so the walk is a
small stack.
An inner descent draws into a different window and must not join the
outer tree, and with a single state its close left the outer descent
with no walk at all: every widget after a canvas built its own tree
again.
That is a batching difference and not a drawing one, which is why the
control for it draws the same pixels and reports 11 widgets batched
instead of 15.
.PP
Painter order is therefore exactly what it was, which is what the
identity check measures;
.B "render count"
answers how many trees and how many widgets, which is what a test asks
to see the batching, since the pixels cannot show it.
.PP
.B TkNglyphs
carries the NAME of its face, not a
.BR Font* .
A font belongs to a display and can be closed under a tree that points
at it, which is why a node holding one could not outlive the call that
built it; a name is a value, and the renderer resolves it with
.I font_open
\(em the same call the toolkit uses for every face it opens, including
.BR *default* .
What it does not yet do is lay the run out into positioned glyphs, which
is the other half of that job and the half a backend needs, since a
backend cannot call
.I string
at all.
.PP
Six node types are declared and not implemented:
.BR TkNtexture ,
.BR TkNlinear ,
.BR TkNshadow ,
.BR TkNclip ,
.B TkNtransform
and
.BR TkNopacity .
They are written down so the numbering does not change later, and
.I tkscenerender
counts them and ignores them.
The last three are the ones that make an animation cost nothing to
rasterise, which is the whole point of the exercise and is not done.
